00:05:09.269 --> 00:05:10.060
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.559
MARK MANDEL: --do you want to
just give us a very high level

00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:14.860
overview of what Kubernetes is?

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:15.610
How does it work?

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:16.110
What is it?

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.255
What problem is it
trying to solve?

00:05:17.255 --> 00:05:18.200
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.840
Kubernetes is a container
automation framework

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.350
based on 15 years of how Google
ran containers internally.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.170
So it's all open source
now, owned by the CNCF.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.880
And what that means is basically
you can set a desired state,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.630
Kubernetes pick up
on that for you,

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.880
and drive your current
state towards it.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.990
So instead of having to, say,
manually run to a command line

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.844
and say, is my
application running,

00:05:38.844 --> 00:05:41.010
Kubernetes will check that
for you, and if it's not,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.290
it's smart enough to restart it.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.206
So this frees up developers
to focus on what's

00:05:44.206 --> 00:05:45.950
really important, applications.

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.880
And I can go more in-depth
if you want me to.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.120
MARK MANDEL: Well, I
guess, it's interesting.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.400
You say applications, but
you don't say containers.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.950
Is there a reason
why you do that?

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.400
CARTER MORGAN: I think sometimes
there can be a confusion.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.780
Sometimes you might
have multiple containers

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.100
that work well together and
that's your application.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.970
I might have an NGINX front-end
that gives me secure routing

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.729
and that's talking to
my standard application.

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:08.270
In a talk I do in
the Udacity course,

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.710
we have this app that
just sends hello back.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.610
So you can bundle up containers
to be your logical application.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:15.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
just mentioned CNCF.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:16.702
What does that mean?

00:06:16.702 --> 00:06:18.660
CARTER MORGAN: I always
get acronyms messed up,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.850
but I think it's Cloud
Native Computing Foundation.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I just check
it out, and yeah, that it is.

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:23.020
CARTER MORGAN: Whew.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:24.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
what does that mean?

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:25.931
CARTER MORGAN: So
what it is, is it's

00:06:25.931 --> 00:06:29.340
a body that's in charge
of Prometheus now, which

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.167
is a monitoring
app, and Kubernetes,

00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:33.750
and it's trying to just drive
it forward and adopt it and keep

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.208
it open for everyone
to contribute.

00:06:35.208 --> 00:06:37.920
So this isn't a
Google-owned project.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.680
Kubernetes is owned
by the CNCF, and that

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.470
lets people like Microsoft
work on it, Red Hat work on it.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.720
And that's what makes it so
valuable to our community.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:46.636
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:48.300
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.300
So let's go back
to Kubernetes 1.3.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.390
You were saying it's twice
the power of Kubernetes 1.2.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:55.655
Why do you say that?

00:06:55.655 --> 00:06:58.190
CARTER MORGAN: Well, I said,
twice as big, twice as big.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.600
Because now in
Kubernetes 1.3, you

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.260
can have up to 2,000
nodes, 2,000 machines

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.750
running your application
and 60,000 containers,

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.810
and that's all still
within the service of SLO,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.800
which is a basically one-second
latency on API calls.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.472
And so in 1.2, there was 1,000.

00:07:14.472 --> 00:07:17.980
So 1.3 is twice as big as 2.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.787
So if that keeps on growing,
1.4 will be 4,000 for the--

00:07:21.787 --> 00:07:22.620
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.770
We're there I just
interviewed someone

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.190
and they said, well, we
want to see 15,000 nodes.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:30.472
And I'm like, you're
not happy with 2,000?

00:07:30.472 --> 00:07:32.680
MARK MANDEL: That could be
a lot of cores, especially

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:35.157
[INAUDIBLE] we can
run 32 core instances.

00:07:35.157 --> 00:07:35.990
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So on
top of being just bigger,

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:39.900
I'm assuming it's also better.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.240
What are the new things?

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.395
What are you excited
about from Kubernetes 1.3?

00:07:43.395 --> 00:07:45.770
CARTER MORGAN: Well, I get
excited about little features.

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:49.695
So even little command line
tools or flags are easier.

00:07:49.695 --> 00:07:51.552
So now there's kubectl set.

00:07:51.552 --> 00:07:54.010
So I can just go in and say,
hey, I want to change my image

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.190
and just change that
one command line,

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.847
which for running demos or
just ease of use, that's nice.

00:07:58.847 --> 00:08:00.930
But there's really big
features that are exciting,

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:06.180
like cluster federation,
initialization containers, more

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.340
security features.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:10.520
So I think it makes sense
to think about Kubernetes,

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.910
it says it's planet-scale,
and you can run it anywhere,

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.400
and you never outgrow it.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.970
And now 1.3, we're actually
really meeting those claims.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.214
With cluster
federation, you can say,

00:08:21.214 --> 00:08:23.380
I want a cluster that's
on-prem and I want a cluster

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:24.910
that's on GCP, and that's fine.

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:26.180
It's perfectly OK.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.138
Or if I want something
that's highly available.

00:08:28.138 --> 00:08:31.650
Well, I want a cluster
that's in an Asian region

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.760
and I want one that's in the US.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:35.100
So now all that's possible.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.675
So the run anywhere
thing, totally you

00:08:37.675 --> 00:08:38.740
can make that happen now.

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:40.573
MARK MANDEL: So let's
talk a little bit more

00:08:40.573 --> 00:08:42.620
about cluster federation
and how that works.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.180
Is this just some
overarching sort

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.390
of abstraction that sits
on top of Kubernetes that

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.790
makes it look the
same across the planet

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:51.990
if I'm going across zones?

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:54.322
Or what does that
really look like?

00:08:54.322 --> 00:08:56.030
CARTER MORGAN: Well,
OK, so basically you

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:57.750
have to set up a control plane.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:01.110
So you set up a
federated service.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.190
And what this does is it
sets up a service for you

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.520
in all the regions
that you want.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.024
It makes sure that
they're healthy

00:09:08.024 --> 00:09:09.690
and that they can
route traffic to them.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.010
It gives them DNS
entries and all that.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.500
You can also do things like
set the context and say,

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:19.184
what region, what cluster
do I want to operate on?

00:09:19.184 --> 00:09:21.100
And then you can manage
it all from one place.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.040
So that's really nice.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.014
So it is kind of sitting
on top of clusters before.

00:09:26.014 --> 00:09:27.930
So now you control all
your different clusters

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.616
from one location.

00:09:29.616 --> 00:09:30.990
MARK MANDEL: OK,
and if traffic's

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.620
coming into that
federated cluster,

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.750
I assume it's intelligently
routed to the least

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.975
latency, things like that?

00:09:36.975 --> 00:09:38.850
CARTER MORGAN: I'm not
100% sure on that one.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.799
I don't want to give a promise.

00:09:40.799 --> 00:09:42.590
But yeah, so you have
the option of hitting

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.104
the federated endpoint
and round-robining traffic

00:09:45.104 --> 00:09:46.520
throughout, or you
can actually go

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:49.950
to the region-specific endpoints
if your application wanted

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.050
you to do that.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:54.180
That is probably the biggest
feature, I would think though,

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.450
of this release.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.080
Because now, as
your service grows,

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.570
you can take it as something
from on-prem, and you can say,

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.530
oh, I want to try this on
an AWS, see how that works.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:04.540
And you could do that.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:07.030
Or if that doesn't work and
you want to switch over to GCP,

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:09.877
and I'm not saying AWS won't
work or anything like that,

00:10:09.877 --> 00:10:11.460
but then you can
switch it over to GCP

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.835
and you don't have to take
everything offline right away.

00:10:13.835 --> 00:10:17.020
You can have a gradual rollout,
still be highly available,

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:18.400
still make sure
everything works,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.714
before you're on-boarding.

00:10:19.714 --> 00:10:21.630
MARK MANDEL: That's one
of the things I really

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:25.290
like about Kubernetes is
kind of the customer wins.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.770
You can run it anywhere.

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:28.590
So you just run around
basically looking

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.580
for the best Kubernetes
experience you can find

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.440
in the place that you need it.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.340
And federation really
seems to sort of fall

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.923
into that as well, because that
gives you so much flexibility.

00:10:37.923 --> 00:10:40.121
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah,
it's really, really open.

00:10:40.121 --> 00:10:42.120
If you look at other
features, the rkt container

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.410
runtime now in 1.3,
that just came out.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:49.200
And so you're no longer
forced to use Docker.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:52.159
Right now, rkt's the only one
we support, rkt and Docker.

00:10:52.159 --> 00:10:54.450
If another container runtime
has features that you need

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.782
or want to rely on,
you can do that now.

00:10:56.782 --> 00:10:58.590
So take rkt.

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.880
It allows you to
run basically images

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:04.020
that are more secure
with a stripped down OS.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:07.670
So you have a VM-based operating
system running your containers.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:10.847
You can also run on host.

00:11:10.847 --> 00:11:11.680
MARK MANDEL: Really?

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.420
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, so you
have host privilege containers

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:14.160
running.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.880
And so if you need that kind of
flexibility and functionality,

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.850
now you have the
option with rkt.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.010
MARK MANDEL: That's
really, really cool.

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.070
Now, I've also heard of this
thing called init containers.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:25.890
This confuses me.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.056
So can you explain it?

00:11:27.056 --> 00:11:28.430
CARTER MORGAN:
Yeah, so basically

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:31.090
how initialization
containers work,

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:33.050
when you have basically
a pod in Kubernetes,

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.710
that's the basic application.

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.169
And a pod is one
or more containers

00:11:36.169 --> 00:11:37.751
that know how to
play nicely together.

00:11:37.751 --> 00:11:38.880
They share an IP stack.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:39.860
They share volumes.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:41.790
They can talk to each
other on local hosts.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.206
But when they start up, they
start at the same exact time.

00:11:44.206 --> 00:11:47.790
There is no guarantee on
pod 1 starts before pod 2.

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.390
Sometimes you need things
to happen in sequence

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.160
before your regular
application pods start.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.985
Say, you wanted to
pull data from GitHub.

00:11:55.985 --> 00:11:58.360
Well, you don't want to write
that into your application.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:11:59.220
That doesn't make sense.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.480
It doesn't fit the application.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:01.610
You just expect the
data to be there

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.580
when your regular
application containers start.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:06.140
So now with initialization
containers, you can do that.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:09.060
What happens is you
specify N number

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:10.480
of initialization containers.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.780
They're going to run in order,
the order you list them.

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.910
If any of those fail, your
pod's not going to start up.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:19.750
If they all work fine, then your
pods, your normal application,

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:22.430
the container pod
will start as normal.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:26.130
So another example why
this would be useful.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.840
Say you have your regular
application container.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:30.795
It does whatever
functionality it did before.

00:12:30.795 --> 00:12:32.920
Maybe you want to register
that with a new service,

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:36.360
an external service, for
monitoring or health checking,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.890
whatever it might be, you
can put in an initialization

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:41.540
container or initialization
step and have it register

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:45.320
your container without changing
any of your application code.

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:47.150
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
is that-- just so I'm

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:49.277
clear as well-- so
that's inside the pod?

00:12:49.277 --> 00:12:51.110
CARTER MORGAN: It's
inside the pod manifest.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:53.330
You write it in the YAML file.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:56.399
So Kubernetes uses a
declarative syntax.

00:12:56.399 --> 00:12:58.690
So you might write down what
you want, the state of it,

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:01.600
and then Kubernetes will
create that state for you.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:03.610
So in that manifest
file, you're right,

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.110
there's a new initialization
container section,

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.342
and you write just the
pod, the templates.

00:13:08.342 --> 00:13:10.300
MARK MANDEL: OK, so you
have a lot more control

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:14.060
now in regards to the order in
which these containers come up

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:16.790
in, like, one versus
another, whereas before, I

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:19.430
think-- I know I've done stuff
where I just run retries.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:21.900
Like, oh, yeah, first time
it fails, back off, back

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.140
off again, back off again.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:24.730
Now you can just be
more explicit about it.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:25.430
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:27.430
And, well, in talking to
people, a lot of people

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.180
say they want to do, basically,
like, serial processing.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.490
So maybe they have some
kind of pipeline going.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.490
They have an application
that they want running,

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:37.060
but they need steps one and
two of the pipeline to run.

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:39.290
Well, now you can do that
with init containers.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.860
I think that's my favorite
feature from Kubernetes 1.3.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:45.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I
was checking on GitHub

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:48.590
and I saw that the issue
called init containers.

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:50.670
It is labeled stateful apps.

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:51.710
What are those?

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:53.927
What is a stateful app
in the Kubernetes world?

00:13:53.927 --> 00:13:56.510
CARTER MORGAN: OK, well, a lot
of times you have applications,

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:59.329
like web servers, you can just
replicate as many as you want.

00:13:59.329 --> 00:14:00.370
They don't really matter.

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:03.734
And you hear people talk
about pet versus cattle.

00:14:03.734 --> 00:14:05.400
So if you have a pet,
you care about it.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.690
If it gets sick,
you want to fix it.

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:07.080
You want to help it.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:08.110
You give it a name.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:10.090
Cattle you're making
hamburgers, man.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.020
It doesn't really
matter what's up there.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.840
So in Kubernetes, what
we have now are Pet Sets.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.830
And basically what this is,
is a Pet Set manages pets.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:21.820
And pets have a unique
name, a DNS record,

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:24.200
and they are associated
with some kind of data.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.572
So if they go down, we
want that to come back up.

00:14:26.572 --> 00:14:27.280
We care about it.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:29.738
We care about the order they
start and the order they stop.

00:14:29.738 --> 00:14:31.780
So that's what stateful
data means in Kubernetes.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:33.571
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I
can imagine something

00:14:33.571 --> 00:14:35.020
like that being
very useful to run

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.912
databases and things like that,
like in SQL with replicas or--

00:14:37.912 --> 00:14:38.870
CARTER MORGAN: Exactly.

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.460
So yeah, there's two types of
databases that you you're going

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:42.820
to see commonly with this.

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.732
There's the quorum type,
like your [INAUDIBLE] kind

00:14:45.732 --> 00:14:47.440
of things, where you
have-- basically you

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.740
need consensus
between multiple--

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:51.360
I can't even think
the word right now.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:52.320
MARK MANDEL: Multiple nodes?

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:53.130
CARTER MORGAN:
Multiple nodes, yes.

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:54.796
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Sounds like Kubernetes.

00:14:54.796 --> 00:14:56.010
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:58.610
And then you also have
something like a MySQL database,

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:02.161
where you might need
to attach one NFS disk,

00:15:02.161 --> 00:15:04.160
and you always want to
be attached to that disk,

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.150
or you always need at
least one pod or node

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.310
attached to that disk.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.892
So these are where stateful
applications come into play.

00:15:09.892 --> 00:15:11.350
MARK MANDEL: Does
this also account

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:13.670
for where you'll always
want something to come up

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.110
in a particular DNS entry?

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.325
Like, this thing has to
always be at this record.

00:15:18.325 --> 00:15:19.200
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:21.170
So you're always going
to have the same name,

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:23.280
because you always want to be
able to access it the same.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:23.880
So yeah.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:24.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.760
So you could put, like, memcache
there or other things like that

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:29.262
as well, where
you're like, I always

00:15:29.262 --> 00:15:30.970
want to be able to
find this in one spot.

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:31.886
CARTER MORGAN: Mm-hmm.

00:15:31.886 --> 00:15:34.620
And so one of the
complaints I've

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.740
heard about Pet Sets-- I've seen
some really cool demos with it.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.590
I just saw a 1,000-node
Cassandra demo.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:40.340
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:41.270
CARTER MORGAN: Very cool.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:42.570
But one of the
complaints is people

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.320
said, well, we could
do almost all of this

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:46.480
before with the API in 1.2.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:48.610
So we've added another object.

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:49.970
Is it necessary?

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:51.992
So it seems like it
makes it a lot easier,

00:15:51.992 --> 00:15:54.664
but there's been a
little contention on,

00:15:54.664 --> 00:15:55.580
is this the right way?

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:57.740
Is this finished yet?

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.830
MARK MANDEL: Well, Pet
Sets is still fairly new.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.970
So I guess there are some--
like, their alpha, if you play

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.650
with them, things may-- not
necessarily things may break,

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:07.401
but things may change
as we go along.

00:16:07.401 --> 00:16:08.900
So there's probably
some wiggle room

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:10.699
there to find the
best case scenarios

00:16:10.699 --> 00:16:11.740
for those sort of things.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:12.614
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:16:12.614 --> 00:16:14.450
And I'm just excited,
because in 1.2, I

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:16.680
was writing a flashcard
app, and whenever

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.470
I had a pod that
would die or restart,

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:21.647
I had to go manually
detach the disk.

00:16:21.647 --> 00:16:22.480
So that's fixed now.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:25.520
It automatically-- they
have detached controllers

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.100
that automatically
unattach your disk

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:29.750
and later reattach when
the pod starts up again.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.000
So that's aside from Pet Sets.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:34.010
So just stateful applications
in Kubernetes 1.3 are better.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:34.735
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:16:34.735 --> 00:16:36.860
And I guess that also ties
back to init containers.

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:38.652
You were talking about
with stateful apps,

00:16:38.652 --> 00:16:40.610
having things come up in
the right order really

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:41.410
is important.

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:41.760
CARTER MORGAN: Mm-hmm.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:43.676
Yeah, so there's a lot
of support around that.

00:16:43.676 --> 00:16:45.367
And Pet Sets, it's
a nice feature,

00:16:45.367 --> 00:16:47.700
and I'm sure we're going to
see a lot more in the coming

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:50.220
months of people using it
and testing it out, seeing

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:51.054
what the limits are.

00:16:51.054 --> 00:16:51.969
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:51.969 --> 00:16:54.150
So we've covered a little
bit about all the things

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:55.580
that you can do,
like more nodes,

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:58.550
and you can also have init
containers and stateful apps,

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.300
and you can run rkt instead of
Docker, and all those things.

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:03.290
Those are amazing.

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:07.930
There's one more thing that I
saw that Kelsey Hightower had

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:09.270
written.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.183
It's "Kubernetes the Hard Way."

00:17:11.183 --> 00:17:12.849
Can you tell us a
little bit about that?

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:13.682
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:17:13.682 --> 00:17:14.520
One, Kelsey's great.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:15.050
Like, great.

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:15.890
He's so good.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:16.349
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We agree.

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:17.390
MARK MANDEL: We agree.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:18.109
CARTER MORGAN: Well,
he's just really

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:20.329
good at getting in the
community and listening

00:17:20.329 --> 00:17:22.560
to what their needs are.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.140
And with Kubernetes, what
we're seeing is a lot of people

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:27.730
think it's really hard to
get started with Kubernetes.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.300
Maybe the documentation isn't
quite there around the steps

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:32.380
to start up a cluster
or maybe there's

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:35.930
a lot of-- there are a lot of
steps to starting a cluster.

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:38.710
So what "Kubernetes the
Hard Way" is is Kelsey

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.120
just wanted to go and
document and say, OK, here

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:45.280
is the steps for a basic
sane default configuration

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:46.900
for starting up a cluster.

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:47.400
That's it.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:48.260
That's all it is.

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:50.700
So now you can go and learn
everything that's involved.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:51.770
MARK MANDEL: I guess
sort of the pain point

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:53.770
before was-- I mean,
GKE is easy, right?

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.640
You push a button and
you get a cluster.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:57.969
There are cube up scripts
to firing up a cluster,

00:17:57.969 --> 00:18:00.260
but I guess that's kind of
a "magic-y," quote, unquote.

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:00.450
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:01.820
It abstracts away the details.

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.020
So you don't know what's
going on in your system.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.230
So how do you adopt
a new framework

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.610
if you don't know even how it
starts up, or how it stops,

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:11.099
or what are the pieces
that interoperate?

00:18:11.099 --> 00:18:13.640
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so I guess
