00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:29.920
documentation around
Kubernetes in general.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:31.336
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So something I've

00:20:31.336 --> 00:20:33.530
seen in many demos that
get people quite impressed

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.900
is when you update from one
image to something else.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:39.320
Migrations c in Kubernetes
are very, very nice.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:41.620
Is there anything new on that?

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:44.761
CARTER MORGAN: Oh, yeah,
there's a new command.

00:20:44.761 --> 00:20:47.260
I think it's kubectl rollout
status now, so you can actually

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:48.724
see what's going on.

00:20:48.724 --> 00:20:50.390
If you've looked at
any of my old demos,

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:52.040
I used to run some
kind of script

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:53.264
and I would manually do this.

00:20:53.264 --> 00:20:55.180
Well, now it's just built
right into the tool,

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.960
so you can see step by
step what's going on.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:59.270
How's your cluster scaling up?

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:00.470
How's it scaling down?

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:01.980
So that's a really nice feature.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:03.188
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's cool.

00:21:03.188 --> 00:21:04.810
So if I'm changing
from one image

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:08.190
to another inside Kubernetes, I
can actually see, very easily,

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.210
what's changing.

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:10.050
Yeah, I've done the same thing.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.299
I've done watch commands
that change every two seconds

00:21:12.299 --> 00:21:15.560
and done get pods just to
see which ones are up or not.

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:16.930
That's super, super handy.

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:19.520
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, there's a
lot of, again, little features

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.280
and little commands throughout.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:22.946
There's a new secret.

00:21:22.946 --> 00:21:24.570
So basically secrets
are sensitive data

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.530
that you don't want to store
in your pod or in your image.

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:30.340
Well, now there's a new one
for generating TLS certs.

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:32.110
So if you want to test
something locally,

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:33.620
you can just use
kubectl [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:35.222
to create some test certs.

00:21:35.222 --> 00:21:37.430
And again, it's that ease
of use that's going to make

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:39.504
Kubernetes the
platform to adopt.

00:21:39.504 --> 00:21:41.420
MARK MANDEL: I'm going
to take the opportunity

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:43.930
to ask a Kubernetes question
since we have you here.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.030
And I'm sure-- this
has been covered a bit,

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.700
but it comes up a lot.

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:50.610
So when do I use secrets and
when do I use config maps?

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:51.980
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:55.182
Config maps are for string
data and they're basically,

00:21:55.182 --> 00:21:57.390
like, so if I had NGINX
config, something that anyone

00:21:57.390 --> 00:21:58.170
could see.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:21:59.890
It's not a big
problem, but it might

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:03.490
vary between two different
runs in my container or two

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:05.980
different environments,
whether it's production and dev

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:07.470
or whatever.

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:10.010
Secrets are your password,
your search history,

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.110
things that you don't
want to hand out.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:12.930
So you're obviously
going to want

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:15.440
to use secrets for information
that you want to keep secret.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:17.607
MARK MANDEL: OK, so if I've
got my database records,

00:22:17.607 --> 00:22:19.856
like, where's my database,
what's its username, what's

00:22:19.856 --> 00:22:21.160
its password, that's a secret.

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:22.950
I want to put that in there.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:25.650
CARTER MORGAN:
Definitely the password.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:27.876
I'd probably use the
username, stuff like that too,

00:22:27.876 --> 00:22:29.000
or an environment variable.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.210
But definitely the
password, you're

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:31.290
going to want a secret for that.

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:31.730
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:34.090
And then so, yeah, something
like an NGINX config

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:35.610
or something like
an Apache config,

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.264
like, whatever, who cares.

00:22:37.264 --> 00:22:39.430
CARTER MORGAN: Now, another
thing that's really cool

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:43.060
is config maps, you can
notify pods downstream

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.430
when configs change.

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:48.680
So if I change my NGINX config,
I can notify a pod downstream.

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:50.540
I don't think you can
do that with secrets.

00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:51.655
MARK MANDEL: I don't
believe you can.

00:22:51.655 --> 00:22:52.150
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:53.320
MARK MANDEL: How does
that notification happen?

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:56.210
Is that, like, files change
or environment variables just

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:57.060
change or--

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.185
CARTER MORGAN: Environment
variables in [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:59.185 --> 00:23:01.740
is my understanding, and there's
a downstream API for that.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:03.120
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:06.200
CARTER MORGAN: But yeah, so
what else, is secrets can also--

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.020
and configs-- can all be passed
through environment variables.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:12.160
Configs can also be passed
command line, I'm sure.

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:14.950
And so you have a lot of
flexibility when using them.

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:16.450
So I try and tell
people to use them

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:17.940
as much as possible, you know?

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.314
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
I was chatting

00:23:19.314 --> 00:23:23.210
with someone about keeping
their WordPress config

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:27.079
and whether they should put that
in a secret or a config map.

00:23:27.079 --> 00:23:29.370
And I think we ended up
deciding to put it in a secret,

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:31.910
because it not only contains
just basic information

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:33.370
about WordPress,
but also username

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:35.411
and passwords for the
database, things like that.

00:23:35.411 --> 00:23:38.520
And that's actually
the wp-config.php file,

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:39.839
the whole thing.

00:23:39.839 --> 00:23:40.880
But that was really cool.

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:42.296
I actually hadn't
thought of doing

00:23:42.296 --> 00:23:44.830
stuff like that to, really,
just even just configure just

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:47.450
generally, like, a WordPress
install or something like that.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:48.283
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:23:48.283 --> 00:23:50.220
Now, what I want
to learn next is

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:52.590
I learn how to use Kubernetes
in production more.

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:54.340
So someone actually
has to take the secret

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:56.140
and give it to the API server.

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.660
So do you use something
like Vault for that?

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:00.746
Where do you store
that secret initially?

00:24:00.746 --> 00:24:01.870
That's what I want to know.

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:02.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:03.928
There's a few different
interesting tools

00:24:03.928 --> 00:24:05.104
that could help you do that.

00:24:05.104 --> 00:24:06.770
I used to do a bunch
of stuff in Ansible

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:07.936
and had inbuilt encryptions.

00:24:07.936 --> 00:24:11.510
So you could do Ansible Vault,
like you were talking about.

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:13.040
Yeah, that's a good question.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:13.670
Excellent.

