00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:30.420
But before we finish
with the episode,

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:32.110
we had this question
of the week, which

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:35.630
is, OK, so with
Kubernetes, it's really

00:25:35.630 --> 00:25:36.892
easy to expose a service.

00:25:36.892 --> 00:25:38.850
And when you expose a
service, basically you're

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:42.480
creating a load balancer
and you get an IP.

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:45.890
Now, you expose that and
people start using that IP.

00:25:45.890 --> 00:25:48.800
And then you change
something, you

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.400
want to migrate to
the new version,

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:54.310
and you need to recreate
the load balancer.

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:57.280
How do you make it so you
will keep the same IP?

00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:59.170
Because that IP is
ephemeral, right?

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.530
I don't want to
change the IP and make

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:06.030
every single one of my
customers, which is, like, two.

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:08.840
Yeah, I don't have
a startup yet.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:11.840
But how do I keep
them from having

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.240
to follow with my IPs changing?

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:15.940
Just, I want that
IP to be ephemeral.

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:17.356
CARTER MORGAN:
Yeah, we don't want

00:26:17.356 --> 00:26:18.910
that DNS to be
changing DNS addresses

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:20.020
and waiting for propagation.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:20.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Not
a very good idea.

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:22.018
CARTER MORGAN: That's no fun.

00:26:22.018 --> 00:26:24.445
So it's actually
really straightforward.

00:26:24.445 --> 00:26:26.100
We were looking at
a blog post that one

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:28.540
of our teammates, Terry
Ryan, wrote about this

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:30.030
and it's really quite cool.

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:32.420
So you're able to--
say, yep, you've

00:26:32.420 --> 00:26:34.890
created that load balancer
and you've got an IP address.

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:37.850
You're able to go into
your console networking,

00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:40.040
look at your
external IP address,

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:42.420
and it's pretty much there's
a little drop-down that you

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:45.381
can change from
ephemeral to static

00:26:45.381 --> 00:26:47.630
and that makes your IP address
static, which is great.

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.040
That makes it belong
to your project.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:50.623
That means you have
it and you own it.

00:26:50.623 --> 00:26:52.310
It's yours.

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.214
What's cool from
that point is OK.

00:26:54.214 --> 00:26:56.130
But what happens if I
delete my load balancer?

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:57.980
I want to make sure
I keep that static IP

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:01.990
address for this load balancer
for this Kubernetes service.

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:04.745
So there's a setting inside
the YAML that you can set.

00:27:04.745 --> 00:27:06.650
It's load balancer IP.

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:09.135
It's pretty much exactly what
you would expect it to be.

00:27:09.135 --> 00:27:10.510
And you can put
in the IP address

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:11.690
that you want it to have.

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:14.450
And if you own that IP address,
it will give it to you.

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:18.110
From there, you can just
kubectl apply the service

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:19.840
and that gives you
the IP address.

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:22.600
From that point forward, if you
want to just delete the service

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:25.534
and then recreate it or maybe
give it to another service,

00:27:25.534 --> 00:27:26.200
you can do that.

00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:27.500
It's pretty straightforward.

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:28.416
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:27:28.416 --> 00:27:31.806
Sounds like the obvious
solution just working,

00:27:31.806 --> 00:27:32.680
which is always nice.

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:33.390
CARTER MORGAN: Which
is really nice.

